{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Avenge the vengeance, etc.</b> The plain sense of the verse would indicate that HaShem imposed upon Moses to take vengeance upon Midian, yet we see that he did not even go out with them to the battle. And although I have explained that the vengeance is the declaration that he would make to them, with all this, the plain sense of the verse implies that he himself would be avenged, not that he would send Phinehas in his place. Further, why did He make the gathering of Moses contingent upon the vengeance of Midian?" "<b>Now,</b> the intent of the statement ״נְקֹם״ admits two approaches: first, it is the arranging of the course of action — meaning, strategizing the order of the battle for the purpose of victory, whether in the required measure or in the arrangement by which he would succeed in destroying his foes and deploying the fighters and so forth. Second, the battle itself, which is the act of vengeance. And Moses understood the correct interpretation, for the intent of ״נְקֹם״ etc. that HaShem said was the strategizing of the order of the matter through whose implementation the vengeance would come about. And the proof for this interpretation is that HaShem did not say to him explicitly ״הִלָּחֵם בְּמִדְיָן״ — which is a definitive expression — and also because he grasped, from what stands before us, that he needed to strategize in the ordering of the matter, for the people upon whom HaShem was commanding vengeance are a people through whom the children of Israel had stumbled in forbidden relations — some in deed, some in thought. And although HaShem struck many of them, ultimately the miracle would be withheld from them on their account, and moreover the accuser would prosecute to cause them to fall in their battles on account of the trace of sin. And this resembles the matter of the calf, where although HaShem struck all its worshippers, with all this HaShem commanded that the High Priest not enter the inner sanctum in golden garments, and He also commanded (Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer, chapter 4) the celestial beings to cover their feet with their wings — because they resemble the foot of a calf — so that the accuser would not be aroused. How much more so at the time of battle, when the accuser would be aroused over the sin that came through them. And go out and learn (Sotah 44a) the measure of sin for which the military arrangers would send men back — and similarly they would be caught even for the slightest trace of an impure thought, and needless to say they would be hinderers of the miracle. Therefore, when HaShem said to Moses ״נְקֹם״ — He meant: the intent of HaShem is surely the strategizing in the ordering of the matter to remove the aforementioned concern, and this is the main thing — that the vengeance will succeed in their hands. And so the servant of HaShem did with his wisdom, as I shall explain below." "And His saying <b>afterward you shall be gathered to your people</b> — it appears that He intended to tell him that beforehand he is not yet complete in the rectification to be gathered to his people, meaning that he would have a hindrance in the Heavenly court that would prevent his soul from approaching the place of its dwelling called ״your people,״ as is known, until He would establish him in judgment — for on everything HaShem brings into judgment and with Him justice is weighed exceedingly. And the hindrance is because this perfect one relaxed in the matter of Zimri and did nothing, as they said of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 20:24) that ״he weakened his hands,״ and HaShem was displeased with him that he did not take vengeance until Phinehas arose. Therefore, when the time of his passing arrived, HaShem said to him: a rectification for this matter in which he had failed — to avenge himself against the doers of wickedness — let him arise and avenge himself against Midian, who had ensnared him; and after he completes the rectification, he would be gathered to his people with no adversary and no etc. And this is among the kindnesses of El and His love for His beloved, guiding them in circles of righteousness." "<b>From the Midianites.</b> He said <b>me-et</b> and did not suffice to say מֵהַמִּדְיָנִים — perhaps He alluded to others besides them who were found among them, namely Balaam, who was found there at that time and whom they killed, as it is written (Numbers 31:8) ״וְאֵת בִּלְעָם״ etc. Or perhaps this is why HaShem hastened the matter before Balaam could return to his land, for he was in Midian only to collect his wages, as they said of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 106a), and he was about to leave — so HaShem spread a net for his foot and trapped him by the avenging sword."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {1 "The acronym ב׳ in the phrase ב׳ דרכים is resolved as שְׁנֵי (two), introducing the two approaches."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:16:13.032869868Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}